  "body": "<p>Dorothea wants to rebuild her mother’s lost teaching. Burn injuries make speaking, moving and accepting a room difficult, but she still cools Kiralyn’s fire, teaches what she knows and asks how to help the doctor who shelters her.</p><h2 id=\"dorothea-room-and-burns\">A room she is allowed to keep</h2><p>Di’trillio and Vessalia give Dorothea a room. She remains uneasy in public, flinching at noise and movement, and movement itself can hurt. Later, walking with Di’trillio, she says she was burned at the stake. She credits a pale woman with saving her; the identity and full act of that rescuer are not established in these conversations.</p><p>When Di’trillio suggests that her growing confidence might eventually make the Pilgrim’s Hearth possible, she hears the possibility of having to leave. He stops to clarify that she is not being asked to go. The room remains available, with salves and help when she is ready. Her gratitude does not turn that reassurance into a completed move.</p><h2 id=\"dorothea-cooling-and-teaching\">What she can already give</h2><p>At the Observatory, Dorothea repeatedly cools the water around Kiralyn with frost. Her own spell movements are stiff and painful. When Kiralyn’s distress turns to rising heat, she still stays close enough to help, then puts her coat around her. Her amulet glows near Kiralyn’s sword, without telling either woman who arranged their arrivals.</p><p>Dorothea explains her mother’s idea of equivalent exchange as a way of understanding magic. She demonstrates healing, then guides Kiralyn in trying to transform her own flame. Fresh wounds close, but the old burns remain. She stops the lesson when Kiralyn hurts her own hands trying to do more.</p><p>Their help continues outside the lesson. Kiralyn shades her from the sun; Dorothea offers cooling and reassurance in return. These scenes supply particular acts of care, while leaving her ongoing pain visible.</p><h2 id=\"dorothea-study-and-pact\">The work she wants to preserve</h2><p>She seeks books and other people’s explanations, hoping to rebuild the journals and teaching she says were all she had left of her mother. Minerva explains a different source of magic: a patron’s shared power. Dorothea asks whether such power can be passed farther on, then listens to the offer of a pact.</p><p>She declines that route. Her preference is to study and preserve what she learns. She asks permission to document the conversation and agrees to keep what Minerva wants private. The planned further discussion in Eovar is still a next step, not a pact or a completed new school of magic.</p><h2 id=\"dorothea-the-doctors-search\">Asking to examine the doctor</h2><p>Dorothea notices Di’trillio’s coughing and asks about his condition. He describes a smog that can collect in his lungs and the mask and herbs he uses to manage it. Before examining him, she explains what her magic would do and receives his permission.</p><p>Her magical examination presents a caustic path through his blood and an animated, mask-shaped presence. This is a vision during the search, not a creature visibly emerging into the forest. She thinks the disease seems aware; it does not respond. Her suggestion that it may be confined to him remains a comforting hypothesis.</p><p>She asks him to stop risking further harm. He will not promise that: he wants to understand the condition and says he has promised Vessalia a future. He does agree to tell Dorothea if it worsens. She hopes to help find a cure. The promise and the search are real developments; a cure is still ahead.</p><h2 id=\"dorothea-a-failed-demonstration\">The silver she remembers making with her mother</h2><p>At the Observatory, Dorothea attempts to turn a wooden statuette to silver. Frost-blue light and a snowflake pattern appear; the grain begins to change. One wrong hand sign breaks the working. The object returns to ordinary wood and Dorothea is left badly dehydrated.</p><p>She says her mother used to change little finds into silver or copper for her. The failure hurts because she wanted to share that beauty. Kiralyn brings water and encouragement. With support, Dorothea also explains her aversion to Clive’s fire; he promises to be more careful. Kiralyn carries her to bed when exhaustion catches up.</p><h2 id=\"dorothea-feather-and-promise\">What she can give in return</h2><p>Kiralyn gives Dorothea fabric for her damaged coat and a warm feather that took effort to preserve. At the Range, Dorothea chooses the feather as a wristband. After struggling with an icy shot, she demonstrates a different working: frost blooms directly on the target. Later she successfully silvers an arrowhead. The earlier failure has not made every attempt impossible.</p><p>Kiralyn’s stronger healing still does not remove her scars. Dorothea admits she has started to accept living with them, then asks for a promise in return: Kiralyn should look forward instead of measuring herself by the elemental knight she did not become. Kiralyn agrees and her sword changes color. Dorothea does not claim to know what that response means.</p><p>She offers to teach Kiralyn more elemental conversion. That offer is a beginning, not mastery already passed on. Kiralyn later asks that Dorothea be allowed to live at the Observatory; Night leaves flowers and a welcoming note. The permission is shown, while the move itself is not.</p>",
